At 17:11 26/09/2002 +0100, Peter Watkinson sent this up the stick:

>What's the difference with Gentoo?

Gentoo is a "built form source" distro - everything, including the initial 
install, is built on the machine to be installed.

The way it works is that there is a"stage 1" tarball that you extract to 
some mount point and then chroot into it, download (emerge) the sources for 
the rest of the kernel and OS and build/install them.

I don't think it's designed as a rock-solid server OS, more along the lines 
of a "tweakers linux" with the latest of everything.

cheers,
Rob

